WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.078 --> 00:00:06.461
On the last day of October, a terrible accident occurred.

2
00:00:06.462 --> 00:00:13.499
Pollyanna was walking home from school when a speeding car hit her.

3
00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:19.581
The driver fled, and no one witnessed the accident.

4
00:00:19.582 --> 00:00:25.794
Luckily, a neighbor soon came upon the unconscious girl.

5
00:00:25.795 --> 00:00:35.667
He carried her home, where a frightened Aunt Polly and sobbing Nancy gently put her to bed.

6
00:00:35.668 --> 00:00:40.999
When Dr. Warren arrived, Nancy ran to tell Tom, who was raking leaves.

7
00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:48.471
Tom dropped his rake. "Is she seriously injured?" His voice shook.

8
00:00:48.472 --> 00:00:52.552
"I don't know!" Nancy sobbed.

9
00:00:52.553 --> 00:00:58.944
"She has a little cut on her head, but Miss Polly's afraid Pollyanna has internal injuries.

10
00:00:58.945 --> 00:01:04.969
You know, Miss Polly really loves that girl."

11
00:01:04.970 --> 00:01:09.303
Nancy ran back into the house to wait for the doctor's diagnosis.

12
00:01:09.304 --> 00:01:17.052
But all she overheard was that Pollyanna had no broken bones and a nurse would arrive that night.

13
00:01:17.053 --> 00:01:20.274
Later Nancy and Tom were eating supper in the kitchen.

14
00:01:20.275 --> 00:01:29.637
"Dr. Warren sounded very serious and said, 'only time will tell,'" Nancy said. "What does that mean?"

15
00:01:29.638 --> 00:01:37.938
Tom sighed. "It means we have to wait."

16
00:01:37.939 --> 00:01:43.604
The next morning Pollyanna woke up.

17
00:01:43.605 --> 00:01:50.654
"Aunt Polly! Why are you sitting by my bed? I need to get ready for school."

18
00:01:50.655 --> 00:01:59.465
When Pollyanna tried to sit up, she fell back on her pillow and moaned. "Why can't I get up?"

19
00:01:59.466 --> 00:02:05.440
"I wouldn't try just yet, dear," Aunt Polly said in a soothing voice.

20
00:02:05.441 --> 00:02:09.152
"What's the matter with me?"

21
00:02:09.153 --> 00:02:14.078
Aunt Polly gave an agonized look at the nurse standing at the window.

22
00:02:14.079 --> 00:02:18.754
The young woman nodded and whispered, "Tell her."

23
00:02:18.755 --> 00:02:22.655
Aunt Polly cleared her throat.

24
00:02:22.656 --> 00:02:29.731
"You were hurt by a car yesterday. But don't think about that now."

25
00:02:29.732 --> 00:02:37.583
"Hurt? Oh, yes, I walked . . ." Pollyanna brushed a hand across her forehead.

26
00:02:37.584 --> 00:02:45.057
"There's a bandage on my head . . . and it hurts!"

27
00:02:45.058 --> 00:02:49.441
"Yes, dear, just try to rest."

28
00:02:49.442 --> 00:03:00.151
"But, Aunt Polly, my legs feel so . . . so odd. I can't really feel them."

29
00:03:00.152 --> 00:03:06.124
Aunt Polly struggled to her feet and gave the nurse a look, begging for help.

30
00:03:06.125 --> 00:03:09.289
The nurse approached Pollyanna's bedside.

31
00:03:09.290 --> 00:03:18.748
"Hello," she said cheerfully. "I'm Miss Hunt, and I'm here to help take care of you."

32
00:03:18.749 --> 00:03:22.415
Pollyanna looked around.

33
00:03:22.416 --> 00:03:28.747
"But I don't want to be taken care of! I want to go to school tomorrow!"

34
00:03:28.748 --> 00:03:36.004
Aunt Polly gave a stifled cry.

35
00:03:36.005 --> 00:03:42.091
"Maybe not tomorrow," the nurse said brightly, handing Pollyanna some medicine.

36
00:03:42.092 --> 00:03:45.386
"Let's see if these pills help you."

37
00:03:45.387 --> 00:03:53.121
"All right." Pollyanna swallowed the medicine.

38
00:03:53.122 --> 00:03:58.900
"But I have to go the day after tomorrow to take exams."

39
00:03:58.901 --> 00:04:03.319
She talked for a few more minutes before falling asleep.

40
00:04:03.320 --> 00:04:08.327
For the next week, Pollyanna slept for most of the time.

41
00:04:08.328 --> 00:04:14.635
When Pollyanna was finally more alert, Aunt Polly explained the accident again.

42
00:04:14.636 --> 00:04:24.073
"So I'm hurt, not sick," Pollyanna said when she finally understood. "Well, I'm glad of that."

43
00:04:24.074 --> 00:04:28.697
"You're glad?" asked Aunt Polly, sitting by the bed.

44
00:04:28.698 --> 00:04:35.039
"Yes, I'd much rather have broken legs than be a lifelong invalid like Mrs. Snow.

45
00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:40.042
Broken legs heal, but lifelong invalids don't get better."

46
00:04:40.043 --> 00:04:45.521
Aunt Polly, who had never mentioned broken legs, suddenly stood up.

47
00:04:45.522 --> 00:04:51.029
Walking over to Pollyanna's dressing table, she picked up a hairbrush and put it down.

48
00:04:51.030 --> 00:04:56.289
She did that with one thing after another for no apparent reason.

49
00:04:56.290 --> 00:04:59.747
Pollyanna watched a rainbow dancing on her ceiling.

50
00:04:59.748 --> 00:05:04.731
It came from the prisms hanging in a window.

51
00:05:04.732 --> 00:05:09.670
"I'm glad I don't have smallpox or measles," she said.

52
00:05:09.671 --> 00:05:14.108
"The doctor wouldn't let you in my room because those are contagious.

53
00:05:14.109 --> 00:05:20.417
And I'm glad I don't have appendicitis—that's painful!"

54
00:05:20.418 --> 00:05:24.657
"You seem to be glad about many things."

55
00:05:24.658 --> 00:05:30.317
Aunt Polly's eyes were filled with sorrow as she lovingly gazed at her niece.

56
00:05:30.318 --> 00:05:38.138
"Oh, I am! Lying here gives me lots of time to think about them and to admire my rainbows.

57
00:05:38.139 --> 00:05:46.996
I'm so glad Mr. Pendleton gave me those prisms! But most of all I'm glad I was hurt."

58
00:05:46.997 --> 00:05:52.162
"What a thing to say, dear!" Aunt Polly exclaimed.

59
00:05:52.163 --> 00:05:56.907
"But it's true." The little girl laughed softly.

60
00:05:56.908 --> 00:06:01.874
"Since I've been hurt, you've called me 'dear' many times.

61
00:06:01.875 --> 00:06:09.469
You never did that before. Some of the Ladies' Aiders used to do that, but it's not the same.

62
00:06:09.470 --> 00:06:16.610
Oh, Aunt Polly, I'm so glad you belong to me!"

63
00:06:16.611 --> 00:06:20.460
Just then the nurse appeared with some medicine.

64
00:06:20.461 --> 00:06:28.807
Aunt Polly, her eyes now filled with tears, fled the room.

